{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-20586",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =0$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ))^{′}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, there is a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14272061/assets/e4eb52fc56e9e94ee9bec84d36269c8c9e0590faf2839f68e8c96bdd18450257.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
